# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  AegisScribe
#  Aspire (.NET 10) · ASP.NET Core · Angular · .NET MAUI · SQL Server · Claude Code
#
#  Two rules of thumb:
#    · If a build produces it, it doesn't belong here.
#    · If it would let someone sign a token, sign a build, or call an API as us,
#      it REALLY doesn't belong here.
#
#  What must STAY committed is listed at the bottom. Read that before adding a
#  broad pattern — a few of those files look ignorable and are not.
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════


# ── Credentials, certificates and keys ────────────────────────────────────────
# First, because this is the one section where a mistake is unrecoverable: once a
# key is in history, rotating it is the only fix. `.claude/hooks/secret-guard.sh`
# stops most of these being written at all; this is the second lock.
#
# OpenIddict signs and encrypts every token in the system. Development
# certificates are generated per machine (AddDevelopmentSigningCertificate) and
# production ones come from configuration — neither is ever a repo file.
*.pfx
*.p12
*.pem
*.key
*.crt
*.cer
*.der
*.jks
*.keystore
*.snk
*.mobileprovision
*.provisionprofile
secrets.json
credentials.json
.credentials.json

# Environment files. .NET user-secrets already live outside the repo
# (~/.microsoft/usersecrets), which is why the AppHost reads parameters from
# there rather than from appsettings — see .claude/rules/aspire.md.
.env
.env.*
*.env
!.env.example
appsettings.*.Local.json
appsettings.Local.json
appsettings.*.local.json

# Blizzard / WarcraftLogs / Discord tokens dumped while debugging.
*.token
token.txt


